														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4435794">
									

<p>I think the interview log could use some reworking to make it more believable. As written it really doesn't sound like a 14 year old to me, or a real human being at all. Like, ask anyone to share anything they can remember about their life, and I just can't imagine them starting with &quot;I used to live in a two-story house. It was a good neighborhood&quot;. That sounds more like a dispassionate, third party account of someone's life than the first thing someone trying to remember their own life would say. Likewise, it strains credulity that one of the very first things the subject would recall is a specific stuffed animal that she had, which also had her name conveniently written on it, a detail that she would feel compelled to mention immediately and unbidden, which hey the interviewer just so happened to have behind his back.</p>
<p>Stuff like that makes it pretty obvious that the dialogue was written with exposition in mind rather than as plausible things a person would say, and that just tears me out of the article.</p>
<p>There are a couple other places where the article's tone is a little uneven, like the postscript about the subject's &quot;mental age&quot;, which is not a thing that can really be quantified to the precision implied by the note, or a few of the notes in the experiment log.</p>
<p>I think you can fix those things up though, so I'm a novote for now.</p>

