<blockquote>
<p>Her dialogue doesn't fit because it's stilted and expositing key bits of information that the author wants to get across to the reader, or clearly setting up the next response from the interviewer.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Sorry if it came off that way but, no, it's stilted that way because this is a kid in the body of a grown woman that crawled out of the ground. She's going to have jarring dialogue because of experiencing something like this. Not to mention she has gaps in her memory between the accident that killed her, and the events that lead up to it.</p>
<p>As for the &quot;expository&quot; parts, I did not at all plan for setting anything up. In fact, I tested it out by saying out loud what I was putting down there for the dialogue to make sure that it didn't sound plain.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>So to me, it feels forced and artificial in the sense that I can clearly see the voice of the author, which isn't something that one can handwave away as being an in-universe mystery. It doesn't really matter who is saying it, because it reads to me as being artificial from the get-go.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Except this dialogue is written in the account of me having to say out-loud first to test if it sounds normal. I needed to think first what kind of words would a young person would say in a situation like this.</p>
<p>I get that this is how it felt to you personally, but i want to be crystal-clear that this was not the intention. Dialogue is difficult to write for, so having to say what to put down in an interview log helps a lot.</p>

